Constructor : constructor is a special method used to initialize 
              an object when it is created.

-->A constructor has the same name as the class.
-->It does not have a return type (not even void).
-->It is called automatically when you create an object using the new keyword.
-->For each constructor we have to create a seperate object

Types of constructors:

1.Default constructor
-->No parameters
Student() {
    name = "Unknown";
}

2.Parameterized constructor
-->Takes arguments to initialize values
Student(String n) {
    name = n;
}

this : a keyword used to refer the current object.

example code of a constructor :

class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    Student() {
        name = "Teju";
        age = 21;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s1 = new Student();
        System.out.println("Name: " + s1.name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + s1.age);
    }
}

working of the above code :

Step 1: Start with Main Class (Entry Point)
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s1 = new Student();  // object creation

        System.out.println("Name: " + s1.name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + s1.age);
    }
}

Step 2: Now Add the Student Class
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
}

Step 3: Now Add Constructor (Important Part)
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    // Constructor
    Student() {
        name = "Teju";
        age = 21;
    }
}

Steps to Create a Constructor
Step 1: Create a Class
        class Student {}
Step 2: Add Variables (Data)
        class Student 
        {
          String name;
          int age;
        }
Step 3: Write the Constructor
        class Student 
        {
          String name;
          int age;
          Student()
           {   // constructor
             name = "Teju";
             age = 21;
           }
        }
-->Same name as class
-->No return type
-->Used to assign values





